1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,704
The school week joins obstetrics, neonates, pediatrics and
tracheostomy care. Each domain still begins with the same question:
what threatens this patient now?

2
00:00:10,704 --> 00:00:21,768
The lessons already covered the seven-E special-populations module,
birth complications, neonatal transition, APGAR concepts and the
assigned tracheostomy topic.

3
00:00:21,768 --> 00:00:32,448
This rehearsal uses different ages and facts from the saved school
cases. The values below are invented training data, not patient
records or assessment answers.

4
00:00:32,448 --> 00:00:44,568
A narrated class cannot certify birth maneuvers, newborn ventilation,
Broselow use or suctioning. Demonstrate each skill on appropriate
training equipment under school supervision.

5
00:00:44,568 --> 00:00:53,520
At a birth, maternal and newborn needs can overlap. Assign separate
attention without allowing either patient’s assessment to disappear.

6
00:00:53,520 --> 00:01:03,432
Name who leads each patient, who tracks time and who calls for
additional resources. Confirm that each request was heard and
completed.

7
00:01:03,432 --> 00:01:12,024
Coordinate with a registered midwife when present. Their legal
professional scope does not automatically transfer to EMS personnel.

8
00:01:12,024 --> 00:01:23,232
A partner should interrupt immediately for an airway, breathing,
hemorrhage or newborn-ventilation concern. Shared situational
awareness outranks a completed checklist.

9
00:01:23,232 --> 00:01:35,184
A twenty-nine-year-old at home reports a strong urge to push. The
presenting part is visible and contractions are close together; there
is no time for a long transport-first debate.

10
00:01:35,184 --> 00:01:44,232
The crew confirms scene safety, introduces its roles and asks the
registered midwife what has happened and what the current plan is.

11
00:01:44,232 --> 00:01:54,816
One clinician continues maternal ABC assessment while a second
prepares a warm newborn area, timer, ventilation equipment and
appropriate sizes.

12
00:01:54,816 --> 00:02:03,048
The training objective is controlled coordination and reassessment,
not a memorized pull or an internal cervical examination.

13
00:02:03,048 --> 00:02:10,416
Ask about the patient’s history and the pattern of contractions while
observing the presenting part and urge to push.

14
00:02:10,416 --> 00:02:16,968
The appearance of crowning or an unavoidable urge to push changes the
transport and preparation decision.

15
00:02:16,968 --> 00:02:24,432
Explain what you are doing, protect dignity and use the appropriate
external support taught in the supervised birth station.

16
00:02:24,432 --> 00:02:33,144
Do not perform an internal examination or use forceful traction to
hurry the birth. The regional procedure and clinician roles govern.

17
00:02:33,144 --> 00:02:40,512
Shoulder dystocia is suspected when the head delivers but the
shoulders do not follow with the usual gentle sequence.

18
00:02:40,512 --> 00:02:49,224
Call for help promptly and follow the current L08 pathway. One person
should track time and support the newborn plan.

19
00:02:49,224 --> 00:02:59,448
McRoberts positioning and suprapubic pressure are distinct external
maneuvers. Demonstrate them only on a training manikin under
instructor supervision.

20
00:02:59,448 --> 00:03:09,840
Do not apply fundal pressure, forcefully pull the head or perform
internal rotation, episiotomy or posterior-arm maneuvers outside the
authorized role.

21
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:17,664
After delivery, continue to assess the patient. Visible blood loss may
understate the degree of physiological compromise.

22
00:03:17,664 --> 00:03:29,088
Describe the patient’s perfusion, symptoms, bleeding and change over
time. A soft or poorly contracted uterus can be one clue, not the
whole diagnosis.

23
00:03:29,088 --> 00:03:38,472
The FourTs framework groups tone, trauma, tissue and thrombin. Use it
to organize questions while maintaining ABC priorities.

24
00:03:38,472 --> 00:03:50,496
Follow current BCEHS L09 authority and consultation requirements. Do
not invent a medication dose, delay support for a volume estimate or
force placental delivery.

25
00:03:50,496 --> 00:03:56,856
Record the time of complete birth and quickly assess tone and whether
breathing or crying is effective.

26
00:03:56,856 --> 00:04:04,992
Maintain warmth and use the correct local newborn sequence. A well-
appearing baby still needs ongoing observation.

27
00:04:04,992 --> 00:04:14,496
If breathing is absent, gasping or ineffective after the appropriate
initial steps, the central intervention is effective assisted
ventilation.

28
00:04:14,496 --> 00:04:25,008
Watch the response, especially heart-rate trend and chest movement. A
minute on the clock is an urgency target, never permission to wait
before indicated breaths.

29
00:04:25,008 --> 00:04:36,624
The saved school outline and station notes say forty to sixty newborn
ventilations per minute. That is the stated school objective and must
remain visible for exam preparation.

30
00:04:36,624 --> 00:04:48,336
The current public BCEHS M09 algorithm specifies a different range.
The current service teaching is thirty to sixty breaths per minute,
with rate selected within the local pathway.

31
00:04:48,336 --> 00:04:58,728
Do not average the ranges, call them identical or teach an invented
hybrid. Effective ventilation, chest movement and heart-rate response
remain central.

32
00:04:58,728 --> 00:05:10,152
Keep the dated conflict visible and confirm with the instructor what
wording the assessment expects. For operational learning use the
current service guideline, not an obsolete number.

33
00:05:10,152 --> 00:05:19,920
Do not compress because the infant is apneic before effective
ventilation has been attempted. Birth resuscitation prioritizes the
ventilation problem.

34
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:28,752
Use current M09 and the selected current neonatal guidance for the
persistent severe-bradycardia threshold after effective breaths.

35
00:05:28,752 --> 00:05:39,744
The saved BCPEG sheet contains an older threshold loop. It is not
silently merged with the current service sequence; the course notes
the discrepancy for the instructor.

36
00:05:39,744 --> 00:05:53,568
When indicated, use the synchronized neonatal ratio and technique in
the current local algorithm. Do not substitute the infant/child BLS
sequence or copy advanced drug boxes into PCP actions.

37
00:05:53,568 --> 00:06:05,088
A seven-year-old had a witnessed convulsion that stopped before the
crew arrived. The caregiver says the child has epilepsy but is unsure
how long recovery usually takes.

38
00:06:05,088 --> 00:06:17,400
The child is drowsy and breathing spontaneously. The crew assesses
airway protection, ventilation, injury, temperature and the recovery
trend before a detailed history.

39
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:30,312
For this fictional practice case, bedside glucose is 2.8 mmol/L.
Record the time, units, method and age; do not make the number itself
a complete diagnosis.

40
00:06:30,312 --> 00:06:42,120
Ask about baseline behavior, seizure duration, rescue plan and recent
food or medicines. Continue primary assessment and seek the current
pediatric pathway.

41
00:06:42,120 --> 00:06:52,536
Appearance asks whether the child interacts in a way that fits age and
baseline. A drowsy post-seizure child still needs airway and glucose
attention.

42
00:06:52,536 --> 00:07:01,560
Work of breathing includes posture, effort, sounds and chest movement.
Rate alone does not show whether breaths are effective.

43
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:12,576
Circulation to skin looks at colour, pallor and mottling as rapid
perfusion clues. They do not replace pulse, blood pressure,
temperature or a trend.

44
00:07:12,576 --> 00:07:21,144
PAT helps identify a child who needs immediate support. It does not
name the cause or replace the structured primary survey.

45
00:07:21,144 --> 00:07:29,664
The saved school outline specifies a glucose threshold at or below two
point six millimoles per litre for children older than one month.

46
00:07:29,664 --> 00:07:39,480
The saved 7E narrative uses a broader under-four point zero statement.
These are not the same threshold or necessarily the same clinical
question.

47
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:50,736
Current BCEHS neonatal glucose guidance is age-specific and must not
be transferred to every child. Use the appropriate current pathway for
the patient’s age and condition.

48
00:07:50,736 --> 00:08:03,480
For an exam, preserve the school value and ask which revision is
assessed. For care, use the current service pathway. Do not erase the
conflict by selecting one unlabeled number.

49
00:08:03,480 --> 00:08:12,024
Lay the child in the position and direction shown by the actual
Broselow instructions; use the device’s correct head-to-heel
landmarks.

50
00:08:12,024 --> 00:08:22,488
The colour zone estimates a weight category and guides equipment
selection. It is not a measured scale weight or guarantee that every
child in the zone is average.

51
00:08:22,488 --> 00:08:33,552
Before any medication calculation, verify the actual medication,
concentration, weight basis, units and maximum dose against the
permitted reference.

52
00:08:33,552 --> 00:08:42,720
The school skill is a supervised tape-estimation station. A colour
band alone does not authorize a medicine or replace training and
scope.

53
00:08:42,720 --> 00:08:53,616
A ten-year-old with an established tracheostomy becomes distressed
after a stretcher transfer. The caregiver hands over a written airway
plan and a labelled emergency kit.

54
00:08:53,616 --> 00:09:06,216
The team checks responsiveness, breathing, chest rise, oxygen supply
and whether an attachment has kinked or disconnected. It maintains
support while one member speaks with the caregiver.

55
00:09:06,216 --> 00:09:15,912
A trained station operator uses the marked catheter, but it will not
pass to the patient-specific depth limit. The learner stops instead of
forcing it.

56
00:09:15,912 --> 00:09:26,304
The team reassesses ventilation, considers obstruction or
displacement, summons advanced airway help and prepares transport
using the current authorized route.

57
00:09:26,304 --> 00:09:33,816
Stop if a catheter will not pass to its measured limit, unexpected
bleeding appears or the patient deteriorates.

58
00:09:33,816 --> 00:09:40,800
Do not force-reinsert a displaced tube or disconnect home ventilation
outside the trained local procedure.

59
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:47,760
Continue the highest-priority respiratory support, ask for trained
help and follow the current service pathway.

60
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:56,808
Report what was directly seen and done. State which airway facts
remain unknown instead of documenting a presumed diagnosis.

61
00:09:56,808 --> 00:10:05,976
Give the maternal and newborn teams separate clinical summaries. Do
not let a single family handover obscure which findings belong to
which patient.

62
00:10:05,976 --> 00:10:13,584
For the mother, include delivery events, bleeding and perfusion
findings, response and remaining concern.

63
00:10:13,584 --> 00:10:23,568
For the newborn, include complete-birth time, initial breathing,
effective ventilation, heart-rate trend, temperature support and
escalation.

64
00:10:23,568 --> 00:10:33,600
Identify the receiving clinician for each patient and confirm that
responsibility was accepted. Continue reassessment until transfer is
complete.

65
00:10:33,600 --> 00:10:40,728
Begin with what the patient’s condition did over time, rather than
whether the learner remembered a preferred mnemonic.

66
00:10:40,728 --> 00:10:48,888
Separate an unobserved skill from a knowledge statement. Saying “I
know suctioning” is not evidence of safe catheter depth.

67
00:10:48,888 --> 00:10:55,920
Ask whether concerns, requests and responses were closed-loop and
whether each patient had a named lead.

68
00:10:55,920 --> 00:11:03,840
Identify equipment or source conflicts without blaming a student for
an inaccessible attachment or undocumented revision.

69
00:11:03,840 --> 00:11:08,952
Explain the reason for the next assessment and how the finding would
change your plan.

70
00:11:08,952 --> 00:11:16,200
Demonstrate only the skills assigned at the station using the
instructor’s equipment, current checklist and supervision.

71
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:25,128
Give a concise but complete maternal, newborn, pediatric or airway
report that distinguishes direct findings from caregiver history.

72
00:11:25,128 --> 00:11:36,912
Name the source revision and role boundary. When a school page
conflicts with current service guidance, document the conflict and
request clarification instead of blending instructions.
